<!-- ═══ FAQ ═══ -->
<section class="tint" id="faq">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Questions</p>
    <h2>House construction costs, answered</h2>

    <div class="faq">
      <details open>
        <summary>What is the house construction cost in Pakistan in 2026?</summary>
        <div class="ans"><p>For standard-quality residential work, gray structure is Rs 2,850 per square foot and standard finishing is Rs 2,800 per square foot, giving a turnkey rate of Rs 5,650 per square foot of covered area. A 10 Marla double-storey house with 3,450 SFT covered area works out to roughly Rs 19,492,500.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How much does a 1 Kanal house cost to build?</summary>
        <div class="ans"><p>At a typical 5,500 SFT covered area, gray structure comes to Rs 15,675,000 and finishing to Rs 15,400,000 — about Rs 31,075,000, or roughly 3.11 Crore, for a turnkey build excluding land.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How much does a 5 Marla house cost?</summary>
        <div class="ans"><p>A 5 Marla double-storey house with 2,000 SFT covered area costs about Rs 5,700,000 for gray structure and Rs 5,600,000 for finishing — Rs 11,300,000 in total, or roughly 1.13 Crore.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is a basement included in the per-square-foot rate?</summary>
        <div class="ans"><p>No. A basement is measured and quoted on its own area at Rs 5,500 per square foot for gray structure and Rs 2,800 per square foot for finishing, a combined Rs 8,300 per square foot. The gray rate is higher than above ground because of excavation, shoring, retaining walls, structural waterproofing and dewatering. Final basement pricing is confirmed after a soil investigation report.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What is the payment schedule?</summary>
        <div class="ans"><p>Six stage-linked instalments: 20% mobilisation advance, 15% at ground floor walls up to plinth, 20% at ground floor walls, 20% at ground floor slab, 20% at first floor walls and slab, and 5% on completion. The same schedule applies to gray structure, finishing, basement and turnkey contracts. Every instalment is released against an inspected stage, not a calendar date.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What is the difference between gray structure and turnkey construction?</summary>
        <div class="ans"><p>Gray structure is the complete structural shell — foundation, RCC frame, masonry, plaster, staircase and water tanks — handed over roofed and watertight but unfinished. Turnkey adds the full finishing package: tiles, marble, paint, doors, windows, kitchen, wardrobes, electrical, plumbing, sanitary, boundary wall, gate, driveway and landscaping, so the house is ready to occupy.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How long does construction take?</summary>
        <div class="ans"><p>A 5 Marla house takes 70 days for gray structure and 50 days for finishing — 120 days, or about 4 months, from mobilisation to handover. A 10 Marla house completes in 180 days, or 6 months. In between and above: 150 days for 7 Marla, 165 days for 8 Marla, 210 days for 1 Kanal and 270 days for 2 Kanal. A basement adds 60 days to any house.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Do rates differ between Lahore, Islamabad and Karachi?</summary>
        <div class="ans"><p>The rates here are national benchmarks for standard-quality work. City-level differences come from freight distance to material sources, local labour supply, water table depth and the bylaws of the governing authority. ACCO confirms the applicable rate for your city and society at the BOQ stage.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What is not covered by these rates?</summary>
        <div class="ans"><p>Land cost, basement, map approval and society fees, utility connections, soil investigation, lifts, solar systems, swimming pools, home automation, air conditioning, generators, furniture, and any specification above the standard finishing schedule. Each is quoted separately once the design is fixed.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I get a detailed BOQ before signing?</summary>
        <div class="ans"><p>Yes. ACCO issues a line-item bill of quantities with measured quantities and rates before any contract is signed, so you can see exactly what each head costs rather than relying on a single square-foot figure.</p></div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

